cp/mv/install of a file that ends in a hole wrote one byte past the end
and ftruncate'd it back.  On AMIX 2.1c that ftruncate (fcntl F_FREESP)
shrinks the file inside its last block, and for a file larger than its
direct blocks (~96 KiB) the kernel panics (grimoire quirk 90).  Measured
on the box: cp --sparse=always of a 110017-byte file whose last 3521
bytes are zero brought the emulated golden 030 down.  Take upstream's
own other branch: seek back one byte and write the NUL there.

--- a/src/copy.c
+++ b/src/copy.c
@@ -389,7 +389,7 @@
 
   if (last_write_made_hole)
     {
-#if HAVE_FTRUNCATE
+#if 0 /* AMIX: an unaligned ftruncate shrink can panic the kernel */
       /* Write a null character and truncate it again.  */
       if (full_write (dest_desc, "", 1) != 1
 	  || ftruncate (dest_desc, n_read_total) < 0)
